  "result": "The command now prints **27.06** (it printed 26.09 before), and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the cents part with a zero on the wrong side. It used `rjust`, so `\"12.5\"` was read as 12 dollars and 5 cents (1205) instead of 12 dollars and 50 cents (1250). Any price with a single decimal digit was being undercharged.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, which pads on the right. A direct check gives `12`\u21921200, `12.5`\u21921250, `12.05`\u21921205 and `0.5`\u219250. For the quote, 2 \u00d7 1250 = 2500 cents, plus 8.25% tax (206 cents) is 2706, which prints as 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. None of the existing tests use a price with one decimal digit, which is how this got through. I didn't add one; a test of `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would cover it.",
